.TH SOCKET 2N UniSoft
.SH NAME
socket \- create an endpoint for communication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <net/socket.h>
.sp
.B s = socket(type, pf, addr, options);
.br
.B int type;
.br
.B struct sockproto *pf;
.br
.B struct sockaddr *addr;
.br
.B int options;
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Socket\^
creates a communication endpoint and returns a descriptor,
much like a file descriptor.  The socket has the specified
.I type\^
which defines the semantics of communication.  Currently
defined types are 
.SM SOCK_STREAM\*S, 
for sequenced, reliable,
two-way connection based streams with an out-of-band mechanism;
.SM SOCK_DGRAM
for datagrams, connectionless, unreliable messages of
a fixed (typically small) maximum length, 
.SM SOCK_RAW
providing access to internal network interfaces.
The type 
.SM SOCK_RAW\*S,
which is available only to the super-user,
is not described here.
.PP
The
.B pf
supplied causes a specific protocol to be used with the socket;
since there is currently only one protocol supporting each socket
type we will not discuss this further.
.PP
The
.B addr
parameter specifies the address for the socket.  A socket
address is a discriminated union with a fixed length of 16 bytes.
The first two bytes indicates the format of the remaining bytes.
The only currently relevant variant is a \fBsockaddr_in\fR, an
internet address.  The first three fields of a variable of
this type are 
.SM AF_INET
(indicating that the address is of the
Address Family Internet, this is defined in 
.RI < \|net/socket.h\| >),
a 16 bit socket number to be used (see 
.RI < \|net/in.h\| >
for lists of well-known sockets), and a 32 bit host address.
The socket number and host address are in network byte order.
.PP
If no address is specified, then the system will assign
one at its convenience; currently it does this at connection
time to simplify the routing decisions required of the connected
socket.
If the socket number is omitted,
a unique socket number will be supplied.
The socket numbers in the range 0 to 
.SM IPPORT_RESERVED-1 
are reserved
for the super-user.
.PP
The procedure
.IR rhost\^ (3N)
may be used to determine Internet host numbers,
while
.IR raddr\^ (3)
converts addresses to standard host names.
.PP
Sockets of type 
.SM SOCK_STREAM 
are full-duplex byte streams, similar
to two-way pipes.  A typical use of such a stream involves creation
with
.I socket\^
and connection to another socket with a
.IR connect\^ (2N)
call,
followed by a sequence of
.I read\^
and
.I write\^
calls to exchange data, followed finally by a
.IR close\^ (2).
Out-of-band data may also be transmitted as described below.
.PP
The protocol used to implement a 
.SM SOCK_STREAM
insures that data
is not lost or duplicated.  If a piece of data for which the
peer protocol has buffer space cannot be successfully transmitted
within a reasonable length of time (typically about 1 minute), then
the connection is considered broken and calls will indicate error with
\-1 returns with 
.SM ETIMEDOUT
as the specific code in the global variable 
.IR errno .
The protocols optionally keep sockets "warm" by forcing transmissions
roughly every minute in the absence of other activity.
An error is then indicated if no response can be elicited on an otherwise
idle connection for a extended period (e.g., 5 minutes).
A 
.SM SIGPIPE
signal is raised if a process \fIwrite\^\fPs on a broken
stream; this causes naive processes, which do not handle the signal,
to exit.
.PP
.SM SOCK_DGRAM
sockets allow sending of datagrams to correspondents
named in
.IR send\^ (2)
calls.  It is also possible to receive datagrams at such a socket
with
.IR receive\^ (2N)
.PP
The primitive
.IR socketaddr\^ (2)
can be used to determine the address of a socket.
.PP
The options available on sockets are ored together in
.IR options ,
and are:
.TP .3i
.SM SO_DEBUG
Enable protocol tracing for this socket, to be used in protocol
debugging.
.TP
.SM SO_ACCEPTCONN
which must be used with 
.SM SOCK_STREAM
sockets which are
to accept connections.  Only sockets which indicate 
.SM SO_ACCEPTCONN
as a creation parameter may do
.IR accept\^ (2N),
and such sockets may not do
.IR connect\^ (2N).
.TP
.SM SO_DONTLINGER
which allows
.IR close\^ (2)
operations on a socket to complete immediately.  Otherwise
the system will block a process waiting for data to drain
(or return 
.SM EWOULDBLOCK
if the socket is marked 
.SM NONBLOCKING\*S)
when a close is attempted.  
See also the 
.SM SIOCSLINGER
.I ioctl\^
below.
.TP
.SM SO_KEEPALIVE
which causes keep alive to be used so as to time out dead connections.
If this option is not specified, then timing out dead connections
is the responsibility of the user process.
.PP
General 
.I ioctls\^
which apply to sockets are:
.TP .3i
.SM SIOCDONE
indicating that the user is done receiving (if the integer parameter is 0),
sending (if the integer parameter is 1) or both (if the parameter is 2)
on the indicated socket.
This is normally used to indicate an end-of-file on a 
.SM SOCK_STREAM
while continuing to read input.
.TP
.SM SIOCSLINGER
sets the linger time to the number of seconds specified by the integer
parameter.
This is currently only partly implemented: linger time is either 0 or infinite
(if non-zero).
.TP
.SM SIOCGLINGER
returns the current linger time.
.TP
.SM FIONBIO
takes an integer parameter saying whether non-blocking i/o is
desired on the specified socket.
Applies to sockets and
specifies that operations are to return 
.SM EWOULDBLOCK
rather than
blocking.  A
.IR select\^ (2N)
operation may be used to determine when i/o is possible without
busy polling.
.PP
The out-of-band data facilities of the stream protocols are currently
primitive, allowing the user
to send a single byte of out-of-band data to the correspondent process.
An 
.SM SIOCSENDOOB 
.I ioctl\^ 
takes as parameter the address of the character
to be sent as a parameter.
This causes a 
.SM SIGURG
signal, indicating an urgent condition, to be
raised in the correspondent process, and places a mark in the data
stream after the last byte written before the out-of-band data was sent.
.PP
The 
.SM SIOCSPGRP
.I ioctl\^
can be used to specify a process group to receive
the 
.SM SIGURG
signal when the out-of-band data arrives.  If the
integer argument to 
.SM SIOCSPGRP
is negative, then it is taken to mean
a single process rather than a process group, given by the absolute
value of the argument.  The 
.SM SIOCGPGRP
.I ioctl\^
returns the current
value of a sockets process group.
.PP
When a process receives a 
.SM SIGURG
signal it can enquire of each of its channels to see which ones
have out-of-band data, by doing 
.SM SIOCRCVOOB
on each channel.
This will return 
.SM EINVAL
if there is no out-of-band data currently
available on that channel.  If a channel has out-of-band data, a
course of action might be to read in the input stream to the mark,
which can be detected by 
.SM SIOCATMARK
which returns a 0 or a 1 into
its integer parameter telling whether the read pointer is now at the
mark.  The system never returns bytes on both sides of a mark with
a single read.
.PP
Facilities to provide the user with interrupts whenever i/o is
possible on a specifiable set of channels are planned.
This will allow interrupt-driven i/o processing similar to the
out-of-band facilities.
.SH SEE ALSO
accept(2N), 
connect(2N), 
receive(2N), 
select(2N), 
send(2),
socketaddr(2N).
.SH BUGS
This call is provisional and will exist in a slightly different form
in future releases.
.\"N	 @(#)socket.2N	 5.2 of 7/12/84
